Requirement Analysis Phase
Legalease – Requirement Analysis Phase

The Requirement Analysis Phase is the stage where the needs of the users and the functions required for the Legalease system are identified, studied, and documented. It helps define exactly what the system should do before the design and development stages begin.

1. Objective

The main objectives are:

Identify the problems faced by users when accessing legal information.
Understand user expectations.
Define the features of Legalease.
Identify hardware and software requirements.
Define functional and non-functional requirements.
Establish the scope and limitations of the system.
2. User Requirements

The system should allow users to:

Create an account and log in.
Search for legal information.
Ask questions using simple language.
Receive understandable general legal information.
Identify the category of a legal issue.
Access relevant official resources.
Use basic document templates.
Generate and download documents.
View previous queries where appropriate.
3. Functional Requirements
1. User Registration and Login

The system should allow users to securely register and log in.

2. Legal Search

Users should be able to search legal topics using keywords or questions.

3. AI Legal Assistant

The system should process natural-language questions and provide general, source-grounded legal information.

4. Legal Issue Categorization

The system should categorize queries into areas such as:

Consumer
Employment
Property
Cybercrime
Family
Civil matters
Criminal matters
5. Document Generation

Users should be able to select suitable templates, enter required details, and generate basic documents.

6. Legal Resources

The system should provide relevant official government and legal-service resources.

7. Query History

Logged-in users can view their previous questions and responses, subject to privacy controls.

8. Admin Management

Administrators should be able to manage:

Legal information
Categories
Document templates
Official resources
User accounts
4. Non-Functional Requirements
Performance

The system should respond to normal user requests within a reasonable time.

Security

User accounts and personal information should be protected through secure authentication and appropriate access controls.

Usability

The interface should be simple enough for users without legal or technical knowledge.

Reliability

The application should provide consistent results and handle errors properly.

Scalability

The system should be capable of supporting additional users, legal topics, and features.

Maintainability

The system should allow legal information and resources to be updated easily.

Accessibility

The application should use clear language, readable text, and responsive layouts.

5. Hardware Requirements
Minimum Requirements
Computer/Laptop
4 GB RAM or higher
Adequate storage
Internet connection
Keyboard and mouse
Modern web browser
Server Requirements

Depending on the deployment method:

Application server
Database server
Adequate storage
Reliable internet/network connection
6. Software Requirements
Component	Requirement
Operating System	Windows / Linux / macOS
Frontend	HTML, CSS, JavaScript
Backend	Python / Java / Node.js
Database	MySQL / PostgreSQL
Browser	Chrome / Edge / Firefox
Development Tool	VS Code / IntelliJ / similar IDE
AI Component	LLM/NLP-based system
7. Data Requirements

The system may need to maintain:

User account information
Legal categories
Legal-information records
Official source references
User queries
Generated-document information
Document templates
Resource links

Legal information should be obtained from reliable and preferably official sources, with source details and update dates maintained where possible.

8. Security Requirements

Legalease should provide:

Secure authentication
Password hashing
Role-based access
Input validation
Secure database access
HTTPS
Protection against unauthorized access
Appropriate handling and minimization of personal data
9. System Constraints

The project may have certain limitations:

Laws and regulations can change over time.
AI-generated information can contain errors if not properly grounded.
Not every legal problem can be handled automatically.
Some cases require advice from a qualified legal professional.
Internet access may be required for certain features.
The availability of reliable legal data can affect system performance.
10. Requirement Analysis Output

At the end of this phase, the project team should have a clear Software Requirements Specification (SRS) containing:

Project objectives
User requirements
Functional requirements
Non-functional requirements
Hardware requirements
Software requirements
Data requirements
Security requirements
System constraints
Project scope
11. Conclusion

The Requirement Analysis Phase establishes a clear foundation for Legalease by identifying what the users need and what the system must provide. The requirements collected during this phase are then used to create the System Design, followed by development, testing, demonstration, and documentation.
